course: dictionary
lesson: 908
title: One word, two birds
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can mean different things in different places. The word blackbird is a good example. Read the clues around it to find out which bird is meant.
explain: «A blackbird tilts its head on the lawn while looking for worms.» A lawn is in a garden, so this is the common garden bird of the UK, Ireland, Australia or New Zealand. Its male is black with an orange-yellow beak, and its female is brown.
explain: In North America, the word can mean several different birds with black feathers, such as the red-winged blackbird. Check the words around it first, then swap in your meaning to see if it still fits.
fact: Blackbird is a noun: a blackbird, or several blackbirds.
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, a blackbird is a common garden bird that pulls up worms on lawns.
fact: The male blackbird is black with an orange-yellow beak, and the female is brown.
fact: In North America, blackbird means several different birds with black feathers, such as the red-winged blackbird.
fact: Blackbirds build cup-shaped nests of grass, moss and mud, and the female lays about three to five blue-green eggs with brown speckles.
exercise: «A blackbird tilts its head on the lawn while looking for worms.» Where is this blackbird most likely to be? | Inside a shop | In a desert, drinking water | *On a lawn, looking for worms | On a mountain top, catching fish
exercise: What colour is the male blackbird in the UK? | Red with a green head | *Black, with an orange-yellow beak | Bright blue all over | Brown with white spots
exercise: What does blackbird mean in North America? | A type of fish | Only the garden bird that pulls up worms | *Several different birds with black feathers | A black cat
test: Nadia says a blackbird in a North American story is the same as the UK garden bird. What should she check? | Whether the story was written on a Monday | *The words around it, such as red-winged, which show a different bird | The number of letters in the word
test: How many blue-green eggs might a female blackbird lay in her nest? | Exactly one hundred | None at all | *Three to five | Ten to twenty
exam: Jayden reads «Four and twenty blackbirds baked in a pie» and thinks it is about the garden bird on a lawn. What is the best thing for him to do? | Skip the sentence and never read it again | Eat the pie | *Notice this is a nursery rhyme, and check what the words around it tell him | Look only at the first word
